1
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:14,778
After the burning of the lac house, the five Pāṇḍava brothers had gone into hiding in the town of Ekacakrā, disguised as brahmins, begging their bread door to door.

2
00:00:14,778 --> 00:00:17,228
But over that town hung a shadow.

3
00:00:21,228 --> 00:00:25,104
In the wood lived Bakāsura, a rākṣasa who fed on men.

4
00:00:25,104 --> 00:00:27,809
Each week the town paid him tribute —

5
00:00:27,809 --> 00:00:34,537
a cartload of food, and with it the one who drove the cart, for none of them ever came back.

6
00:00:34,537 --> 00:00:41,411
Now the lot had fallen on the very house that had sheltered the brothers, and the family wept.

7
00:00:45,611 --> 00:00:52,693
Then Kuntī spoke, quiet and certain: "Do not grieve. You gave my sons shelter —

8
00:00:52,693 --> 00:00:59,954
now my son will take your place." And she chose Bhīma, the strongest of the five.

9
00:01:04,154 --> 00:01:08,345
At dawn Bhīma drove the loaded cart toward the forest —

10
00:01:08,345 --> 00:01:14,670
and being Bhīma, and being hungry, he ate the demon's dinner himself along the way.

11
00:01:18,670 --> 00:01:25,541
The rākṣasa came roaring from the trees — huge as a hill, furious at the empty cart.

12
00:01:25,541 --> 00:01:32,903
They fought, tree against fist, until Bhīma seized him and broke his back across his knee.

13
00:01:38,303 --> 00:01:46,497
In the morning the people of Ekacakrā found the demon dead at their gates, and wondered who had saved them.

14
00:01:46,497 --> 00:01:48,948
The brahmins kept their secret —

15
00:01:48,948 --> 00:01:55,151
and walked on quietly, toward Pāñcāla, and a destiny that would change the world.
